\begin_layout Subsection
Overview of the Focussed Ion Beam dual-beam instrument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Describe the column first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unlikely history of the focussed ion beam (FIB) seems unconventional
 when compared to complementary techniques such as the TEM and scanning
 electron microscope (SEM).
 The liquid-metal ion source span out of electrostatic rocket engine development
 in the early 1960s and are still used in spacecraft micropropulsion systems
 (e.g.
 the NASA/ESA LISA Pathfinder
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lisalaser"

\end_inset

) due to their fast response time and fine current control.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wright2015electric"

\end_inset

 Further development by various academic institutions lead to the first
 scanning ion microscopes (SIMs, not to be confused with secondary ion mass
 spectrometry; SIMS) using 25 keV gallium ions to achieve resolutions of
 0.2 μm (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "Figure 1.1.1"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "orloff1993highresolution"

\end_inset

 By the end of the 1980s FIBs were being used routinely in the semiconducting
 industry for failure analysis, circuit modification and lithographic mask
 repair.
 They were relatively simple to construct as a single column, with the high
 quality Wien filtered (i.e.
 energy filtered) beam adaptable to a large range of working distances.
 As such they could be bolted to existing vacuum chambers and readily adapted
 to provide complementary microscopy and surface analysis.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bassim2014recentadvances"

\end_inset

 A popular combination was with SEM and became known as a dual-beam instrument.
 This success is due to the complementary properties of the FIB and SEM.
 LIST THESE information due to their differing contrast mechanisms.
 
\end_layout

